---
title: Blanched green beans
description: Lesson 0.4. Boiling a green vegetable so it stays green, and how much salt that takes.
servings: 4
time: 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, basics]
lesson: "0.4"
technique: blanching and shocking
status: draft
---

> Technique: blanching and shocking. Blanching is a short boil in
> salted water that sets a vegetable's colour, flavour and texture.
> Shocking is a plunge into ice water that stops the cooking at once.

Fill a #large bowl{} with cold water and @ice{2%handfuls}.

Bring @water{2%litres} to a rolling boil in a #large pot{} and add @salt{60%g}. Yes, that much.

> Why so much salt: in a side-by-side test, beans boiled in water this
> salty were seasoned all the way through, were tender five minutes
> sooner, and stayed bright green, while beans from lightly salted
> water had faded to a drab olive.
-- source: Andrew Janjigian, "The Best Way to Boil Green Beans", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-29, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1558-the-best-way-to-boil-green-beans (a quarter cup of salt in 2 quarts of water)

> Science: the walls of plant cells are held together by pectin, and
> pectin chains are cross-linked by calcium ions. In strongly salted
> water, sodium ions take the place of some of that calcium, the
> cross-links cannot form, and the vegetable softens faster. This is
> Harold McGee's explanation, quoted by the test kitchen; they did not
> test the mechanism themselves. Look up: pectin, calcium
> cross-linking, cell wall.
-- source: Andrew Janjigian, "The Best Way to Boil Green Beans", America's Test Kitchen, 2019-05-29, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1558-the-best-way-to-boil-green-beans

Add @green beans{400%g}(stalk ends trimmed). Boil for about ~{5%minutes}, tasting one from the fourth minute, until they are tender with a little bite left.

> Why the lid does not matter: an old rule says to leave the pot
> uncovered so acids can escape. The test kitchen blanched batches
> both ways and found no difference, in the vegetables or in the
> acidity of the water.
-- source: "Why and How to Blanch and Shock Vegetables", Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/5506-why-and-how-to-blanch-and-shock-vegetables

Lift the beans out with a #slotted spoon{} straight into the ice water. Leave them for ~{2%minutes}, until cold, then drain and pat dry.

> Why ice water: it stops the cooking immediately, which keeps the
> colour bright and the texture crisp, and it rinses off the salty
> water.
-- source: "Why and How to Blanch and Shock Vegetables", Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/5506-why-and-how-to-blanch-and-shock-vegetables ; "The Best Way to Blanch Vegetables", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/13338-best-ever-blanched-vegetables

Toss the beans with @olive oil{1%tbsp} and serve, cold or warmed through in a pan.

> Next time, without the recipe: a big pot of boiling water as salty
> as the sea, a short boil, a taste, and ice water. Asparagus,
> broccoli florets and sugar snap peas take 2 to 3 minutes.
-- source: "The Best Way to Blanch Vegetables", America's Test Kitchen (as above)
